WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
Why does a ganglion cell answer a small spot better than light spread over the whole

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.420
retina?

3
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:14.703
In 1953, Stephen Kuffler recorded single ganglion cells in cats.

4
00:00:15.663 --> 00:00:17.723
He shone small spots of light onto the retina.

5
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:22.197
A spot in the middle of the field made one cell fire faster.

6
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.810
A spot on a ring around the middle made the same cell fall silent.

7
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.050
When the light went off, it fired.

8
00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.773
That is an ON-centre cell.

9
00:00:32.553 --> 00:00:34.613
An OFF-centre cell does the mirror image.

10
00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:39.733
The centre comes from the bipolar cells that contact the ganglion cell.

11
00:00:40.593 --> 00:00:42.593
The surround comes from cells that act sideways.

12
00:00:43.716 --> 00:00:48.816
Horizontal cells do this in the outer retina, and amacrine cells add more in the

13
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:52.956
inner retina. Both push the response the opposite way from the centre.

14
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Now light the whole field, centre and surround together.

15
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.500
More spikes than the best spot, fewer, or no change from the background?

16
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.890
In this example, fewer: 45 spikes a second instead of 60.

17
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.110
Its surround cancels only part of the centre.

18
00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:20.943
So the cell reports contrast, a spot brighter than its surroundings, more than

19
00:01:20.943 --> 00:01:21.963
overall light level.

20
00:01:22.966 --> 00:01:27.426
Now line up a row of ON-centre cells across an edge, where light meets dark.

21
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:30.716
The cell right on the edge is not the winner.

22
00:01:31.616 --> 00:01:34.416
In this model, cells just on the bright side fire most.

23
00:01:35.356 --> 00:01:37.996
Their surrounds reach into the dark and subtract less.

24
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:41.253
Cells just on the dark side fire least.

25
00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:44.013
The edge stands out across the population.

26
00:01:45.116 --> 00:01:46.756
The same logic gives colour.

27
00:01:47.676 --> 00:01:51.376
One cone cannot tell a change in wavelength from a change in intensity.

28
00:01:52.466 --> 00:01:57.306
Compare two cones with overlapping curves, and their ratio changes with wavelength.

29
00:01:58.346 --> 00:02:00.106
Turn the light up, and the ratio holds.

30
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.830
But in pigment models the ratio can repeat.

31
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.310
Set two wavelengths to the same L catch, and their M catches match too.

32
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.770
The S cone catches far more of the shorter one, so it tells them apart.

33
00:02:13.783 --> 00:02:15.103
Here is the whole rule.

34
00:02:15.923 --> 00:02:19.023
One receptor cannot separate wavelength from intensity.

35
00:02:19.943 --> 00:02:20.943
The circuit compares.

36
00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:26.233
The takeaway: ganglion cells send those comparisons to the brain as spikes.
